            <ArticleTitle>Evaluation of abdominal pain: clinicians' performance compared with three protocols.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>793-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Clinical findings in ambulatory patients with abdominal pain, presenting themselves to general medical practitioners, were analyzed by three different protocols: an algorithm using a branched-chain logic, a linear discriminant rule, and bayesian analysis. All methods missed some serious diagnoses. None was as effective as the clinician at distinguishing nonspecific abdominal pain (NSAP) from more serious conditions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
